WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.520
Welcome to the Reason Interview.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.058
Well, thank you very much.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:18.159
Tonight's guest is Annie Duke,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.139
a former professional poker player

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.669
who holds a bracelet from the World

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.379
Series of Poker that you got in

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.550
2004.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.279
She's the author of several books,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.809
including "Thinking in Bets", which

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.819
I interviewed her about for Reason

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.769
a few years ago, and many of the

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.449
insights that I gained both from

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.709
reading that book and talking with

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.599
you, they still come up

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.219
when I'm making bad decisions.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.139
I know why I'm making the wrong

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.149
decision, but thinking in bets.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.279
And last year's book "Quit:

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.289
The Power of Knowing When to Walk

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.939
Away". She holds a Ph.D.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.649
in cognitive psychology from the

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.479
University of Pennsylvania,

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.609
earning her degree after taking a 30

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.379
year break from academia.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.999
We're going to talk about all that,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.989
and more.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:00.639
Annie, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.319
Thank you for having me.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.329
Let's start by discussing Quit.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.159
You write at the start that

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.409
while grit is a virtue,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.989
quitting is a vice.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.059
Can you explain that and summarize

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.409
the book's message?

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.919
Sure. So let me be clear.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.519
I don't think that quitting is a

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.529
vice, but I think that people

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.629
generally think that, you know, grit

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.489
is the sort of heroic

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.319
act, and people who

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:27.639
quit lack courage.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.839
And we know this because there's

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:29.859
lots of aphorisms.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:30.879
You know, winners never quit.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.899
Quitters never win.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.919
First you don't succeed.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.639
Try, try again.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:34.693
So and so.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.669
My parents, to

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.499
inject my biography into

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:39.579
this conversation, these

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.439
conversations, which I like to are

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.369
buried in Mt.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.019
Olivet Cemetery, which is the same

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.269
same cemetery that Vince Lombardi

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:47.499
is buried in.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.599
So I think about him a lot.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.609
And my favorite aphorism

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.499
of his is, show me a good loser

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.849
and I'll show you a loser.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:55.269
Yeah.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.099
Which is adjacent

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.359
to this topic.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.360
It is. It's quite adjacent to this
topic.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:01.629
Winning isn't everything. Winning is

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:02.564
the only thing, you know.

00:02:02.565 --> 00:02:04.989
Quitters never, etc...

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.119
So quitting is a vice.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.219
Right? So the the

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.659
point that I'm making in the book is

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:11.660
that grit

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:13.929
and quit are really the same

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.359
decision. If we think about

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.799
what makes grit good, it's the

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.929
ability to stick to things

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.909
even if they're hard that

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.139
are worthwhile.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.119
And what we forget is the worthwhile

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.189
part. So we just think, "Oh,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.599
we should stick to hard things", and

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.129
somehow that's a sign of character.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.329
But obviously, that's patently

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.159
ridiculous. If you're climbing Mount

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.079
Everest in a snowstorm and

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.579
a blizzard comes in, you shouldn't

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.139
continue to the summit.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.009
That would be silly.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.241
Although people do that all the

00:02:40.242 --> 00:02:40.243
time, right?

00:02:40.244 --> 00:02:41.244
They do because

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.259
we have a bias toward grit.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.969
So what we want to remember is this

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.199
worthwhile piece.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.059
Right. So if something is no longer

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.399
worthwhile, quitting is actually

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.389
not a vice.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.339
It's just as much of a virtue

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.789
as sticking to things is.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:56.919
Because they're the exact same

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.809
decision. Any time that you have

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.459
started something, you have a

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:01.689
choice. Should I continue it?

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.699
That would be grit or should I quit

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.709
it? That should be quitting.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.689
Has there ever been a time, and

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.069
I don't... you know, modern history.

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.079
In the past 500 years where people

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.819
were like, "Yeah, you know, quitting

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.259
is a virtue".

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.699
No, no.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.559
We have a very, very strong bias

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.299
toward thinking that grit is good.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.189
And it's a little bit of

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:22.419
a...

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.679
It's like a chicken and the egg

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.629
issue in the sense

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.519
that we can

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.439
think like, is it this bias

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:30.440
toward,

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.889
you know, is it sort of the cultural

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.809
aspect of, you know, like the Vince

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.879
Lombardi and so and so forth,

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.799
that's making us then be very

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.569
biased toward sticking to things?

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:42.369
Or is it that we're naturally biased

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.329
towards sticking things?

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.859
And then the aphorisms kind of

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.779
follow and we don't

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.579
know. We can't really untangle that.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.559
But what we do know is that you

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.029
can look at a whole host of

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.800
cognitive biases which are just

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.439
errors that we make in our thinking

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.449
that come from shortcuts that

00:03:58.450 --> 00:03:59.740
have kind of gone awry.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.919
And there's just a huge

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.719
set of biases that all kind of

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.059
result in a failure to stop things,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.859
to stick to things too long.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.839
And we'll get into some of those.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.999
At the start of the book, you

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.829
invoke Muhammad Ali, who's almost

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.328
certainly the greatest boxer of all

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.458
time. And certainly, he's famous

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.078
for coming back multiple times to

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:22.149
win the crown again and again.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.099
What's the lesson you want people

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.009
to take from Muhammad Ali's

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:27.969
grit?

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.589
Yeah. So, I mean, I think that

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.809
Muhammad Ali is such a good

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.699
example of this

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:34.959
dichotomy, right?

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.789
This sort of like battle

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.549
between grit and quit, because

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.229
as many of you know, born Cassius

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.809
Clay beat Sonny Liston

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.819
in order to gain the title,

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.350
converted to become

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:50.829
a muslim, changed his name

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.359
to Muhammad Ali.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.589
And then when the Vietnam War

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:54.789
happened and he was supposed to be

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:56.499
drafted, he became a conscientious

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:57.500
objector.

